10. Step 3 — Define the Peer Group

Relative performance must be calculated against teammates doing comparable work.
Recommended Peer-Group Hierarchy:


LOB → Tenure → Channel → Reporting Period
Example: A Premium Chat teammate within 6-90 months should be compared with other comparable Premium Chat teammates within the same tenure band rather than with the rest of Core or Premium.
11. Minimum Peer Population

Relative ranking becomes less reliable when the population is too small.
Recommended Minimum: 20 eligible teammates.
If fewer than 20 teammates exist in the defined peer group:


Do not create an unreliable percentile.
Use the approved fallback peer group.
If no valid fallback exists, mark Relative Performance as N/A.
Apply the approved alternative scoring treatment.
The fallback hierarchy must be documented and approved before implementation.
12. Step 4 — Calculate Reverse Percentile

PACE uses a reverse percentile convention.
PACE Ranking Direction:


0 = Best
100 = Worst
This is intentionally the reverse of conventional percentile interpretation.
It makes the ranking easier to read: Lower number = better performance.
Higher-is-better KPIs (Smiley, AFTR, Quality):


Rank teammates from: Highest actual → Lowest actual
The highest performer receives the best reverse percentile.
Lower-is-better KPIs (AHT):


Rank teammates from: Lowest actual → Highest actual
Best performer = lowest percentile number.
The logic: Doing this primes us to compute percentiles in reverse similar to how traditional rankings are done.
