31. Historical Back-Testing

Before PACE is implemented operationally, Reporting must back-test the model using historical data.
Recommended period: "6–12 months"
Purpose: Determine whether the model produces sensible results.
Reporting should compare:


Existing performance classifications
New PACE classifications
KPI distributions
PACE distribution
Target attainment
Relative ranking
Outliers
Unexpected classifications
Review criteria: The team should specifically look for cases where: "A person who is below target receives a high PACE classification." Any such case must be reviewed.
32. PACE Distribution Review

PACE should not be designed to force: "25% P / 25% A / 25% C / 25% E"
That would recreate the problem PACE is designed to solve.
PACE distribution should reflect actual account performance.
If 40% of the population meets Peak criteria, the model may legitimately produce 40% Peak.
If only 5% meet Peak criteria, it should produce approximately 5%.
